\fBnologin\fR displays a message that an account is unavailable and
exits non-zero. It is typically used as a replacement login shell to
deny interactive access to an account, while still allowing services
that don't require a real shell (cron jobs, FTP, etc.) to function.
.PP
If the file \fI/etc/nologin.txt\fR exists, its contents are printed in
place of the default message. This lets administrators provide an
explanation (e.g., scheduled maintenance) without changing the binary.
.PP
\fBnologin\fR ignores any options or arguments passed to it; in
particular, the shell-compatibility flags \fB\-c\fR, \fB\-i\fR,
\fB\-l\fR, and \fB\-\-rcfile\fR are accepted and silently discarded so
that programs invoking it as a shell don't error out.
